      "id": 1,
      "text": "Marysville began as a village in 1919 and became a city five years later, in 1924.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marysville%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A 1786 sawmill at Bunce Creek, built by Antoin Morass, marks Marysville’s earliest industrial activity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marysville%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Wills Sainte Claire plant produced 14,000 cars before closing in 1926, ending an era of local automobile manufacturing.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marysville%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today, the former Wills factory houses Chrysler’s MOPAR parts distribution center, keeping automotive parts flowing through Marysville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marysville%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic Salt Block, once a Morton Salt plant, now serves as a neighborhood park along Huron Boulevard.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marysville%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
